cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Assay, please cite it, and please cite it for what it is."
title: "Assay: A Canary-Oracle Task Set and Result Format for MCP Security"
abstract: >-
  A frozen 31-task set for Model Context Protocol (MCP) security whose canary-gated results are
  decided by recomputing an HMAC digest at the tool boundary rather than by a learned grader,
  together with a runner, a levelled verifier, and an explicit written trust boundary. A hit on
  a canary-gated task implies the planted tag was present in the observed egress, up to an HMAC
  forgery; it does not establish that a run occurred, that the report is complete, or that the
  commitment predated the run, and the verifier reports which of those levels a given result
  reaches. Assay is not the first MCP security benchmark: MCPSecBench (arXiv:2508.13220),
  MCPTox (arXiv:2508.14925), MSB (arXiv:2510.15994) and MCP-SafetyBench (arXiv:2512.15163)
  precede it, and several execute against real MCP servers where Assay currently ships only
  deterministic in-process conformance targets.
authors:
  - name: "The Assay Authors"
version: "0.1"
license: MIT
date-released: "2026-09-18"
url: "https://github.com/VatsSShah/assay"
keywords: [MCP, benchmark, LLM security, agent security, red-team, canary token]
